WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.243
US tariff impact twenty twenty-five.

00:00:03.193 --> 00:00:05.636
Canada's economic exposure.

00:00:05.636 --> 00:00:10.818
Seventy-five percent of Canadian exports go to one country: the United States.

00:00:10.818 --> 00:00:15.477
Over seven hundred billion dollars in annual bilateral trade crosses the border.

00:00:15.477 --> 00:00:21.874
When the US imposes tariffs on Canadian goods, the Canadian economy has no comparable alternative market.

00:00:21.875 --> 00:00:27.011
The auto sector, energy exports, agriculture, and lumber are all exposed.

00:00:27.011 --> 00:00:29.477
This is not a new vulnerability.

00:00:29.477 --> 00:00:34.204
Canada's economic dependence on the US market has been documented for decades.

00:00:34.204 --> 00:00:39.477
But the tariff escalation in twenty twenty-five has exposed this dependence with new urgency.

00:00:39.477 --> 00:00:45.193
The question for the institutional capture thesis is: whose interests does the Canadian response serve?

00:00:45.193 --> 00:00:48.045
The PM came from global finance.

00:00:48.045 --> 00:00:52.477
The response prioritises trade relationships with global institutions.

00:00:52.477 --> 00:00:57.954
The workers in auto plants, oil fields, farms, and lumber mills bear the economic pain.

00:00:57.954 --> 00:01:01.590
The institutions that produced the PM operate globally.

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:04.499
The workers who elected him operate locally.

00:01:04.499 --> 00:01:08.068
Four sectors most exposed to US tariff impact.

00:01:08.068 --> 00:01:10.931
Why Canada is structurally vulnerable.

00:01:10.931 --> 00:01:13.636
The vulnerability is not the tariffs.

00:01:13.636 --> 00:01:18.170
It is the decades of policy decisions that created dependence on one market.

00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:21.249
The tariff crisis and institutional capture.

00:01:21.249 --> 00:01:26.170
The tariff crisis exposes the institutional capture architecture in real time.

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:30.568
The PM's response prioritises global institutional relationships.

00:01:30.568 --> 00:01:32.590
The workers bear the pain.

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:36.920
Corporate welfare flows to multinationals while small businesses close.

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:45.590
The Bank of Canada must respond to the economic downturn with monetary policy — the same fiscal-monetary tension documented in the Bank of Canada analysis.

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:54.431
Defence spending increases are announced under US pressure — but the military documented in the military purge analysis cannot effectively deploy regardless of budget.

00:01:54.431 --> 00:02:03.488
And the media captured by government funding reports the crisis through the lens of the institutional response rather than the structural vulnerability that decades of policy created.

00:02:03.488 --> 00:02:07.329
The tariff crisis is not separate from institutional capture.

00:02:07.329 --> 00:02:14.465
It is the moment when captured institutions must perform under pressure — and the performance reveals whose interests they actually serve.
